<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  What the app is allowed to talk to in the clear.

  Android has blocked plaintext HTTP by default since API 28, and this app's
  backend is a Flask server with no TLS at all. Without a policy the release
  build silently fails every request: `flutter run` works, the APK does not, and
  the symptom is a connection error that looks like the server being down.

  The policy is deny-by-default with two exceptions, rather than the usual
  `android:usesCleartextTraffic="true"`. That flag permits plaintext to ANY
  host, forever, including whatever production turns out to be — it is a
  one-word way to ship the app's credentials over an open network.

  THE TWO EXCEPTIONS ARE BOTH LOOPBACK, and that is what makes them safe: traffic
  to them never leaves the handset.

    127.0.0.1 / localhost   `adb reverse tcp:5000 tcp:5000` forwards the phone's
                            own loopback to the development machine over USB.
                            This is how a physical device reaches a local server
                            without either of them being on a trusted network.

    10.0.2.2                the Android emulator's alias for its host's
                            loopback. See AppConfig.defaultBaseUrl.

  A development machine's LAN address is deliberately NOT listed. It changes
  with every network, so it would be a line in this file that is wrong more
  often than it is right — and permitting cleartext to a routable address is
  exactly the case this config exists to prevent. Use the adb tunnel.

  WHEN THE SERVER GETS TLS: nothing here changes. `https://` is already
  permitted everywhere by the base config.

  WHEN THE SERVER IS DEPLOYED WITHOUT TLS: do not add it here. The app carries a
  session cookie for police, judges and lawyers; over plaintext on a real
  network that cookie is readable by anyone on the path. Terminate TLS in front
  of Flask instead.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" />

    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">127.0.0.1</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">localhost</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">10.0.2.2</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
